    "terminal_reason": "completed",
    "result_text": "I implemented `check_server(server, cases)` in `mcpcheck/inproc.py`. All 3 tests pass, including a new one that runs it against a real `MCPServer`.\n\n**How it works:** it opens `mcp.Client(server, mode=\"legacy\")`, which runs the server in a background task and connects to it through in-memory streams, so there's no subprocess and no sockets. It then does what `check_command` does: it calls `session.call_tool(case.tool, case.arguments)` on the underlying `ClientSession` for each case in order and runs each result through the same `compare()`. It returns the mismatches in case order.\n\n- **Why `mode=\"legacy\"`:** by default, mcp 2.2's `Client` talks to an in-process server directly, skipping JSON-RPC framing and the initialize handshake. Legacy mode keeps both, so the in-process path behaves the same as the stdio path.\n- **Why `Client`:** I used it instead of building the session myself because the SDK's in-memory transport is private (`mcp.client._memory`), and connecting an `MCPServer` by hand would mean reaching into its private `_lowlevel_server`. `Client` is the SDK's public way to do this.\n- **Type hint:** `server` is typed as `MCPServer`, the new name for `FastMCP` in mcp 2.2. At runtime a low-level `Server` would also work.\n- **Dependencies:** none added.\n\n**Test:** `tests/test_inproc.py` builds a small server with `@server.tool()` (`add`, `greet`) and runs four cases, two of which fail on purpose. It checks that exactly those two mismatches come back, in order, with the server's actual output. It uses `anyio.run`, which is already installed with mcp, so it needs no async pytest plugin."
  },
